I think blessing should be left to the gods and holy water. It's fairly
easy to bless currently anyway.

As for sorceries and their ingredients it might be fun to design a
meaningful system. For example newts have magical energy (mild buzz) so
could be used to enchant +1 (only when fresh to balance things--perhaps
use the same check as #offering). Bats, a traditional ingredient, are
confusing and might be used for fooproofing (but not repairing) if
boiled in a (lit) potion of oil with some floating eyes to make (freeze)
the change permament. A wormtooth in conjunction with fresh newts might
enchant weapons (the weapon you are wielding) but not armor.

For example a system may require for sorceries 3 ingredients:

- a fixing ingredient: a floating eye, a gelatinous cube, etc.
- an energy ingredient: fresh newt, !oGE, etc.
- an active ingredient defining the action: wormtooth--enchant weapon,
dragon scales--enchant armor, bat (perhaps a specific kind)--fooproof..

The frequencies of ?oEW/?oEA may be reduced in favour of sorcery books
which have recipes. It might be interesting if sorcery requires
different ingredients every game and thus the text on the books is
always deffernt? There should also be some stats requirements (Dex,
Wis?) for a 100% successful sorcery--an unsuccessful one could cause an
explosion/a curse/cancellation.

Sorceries which come to mind:

cure sickness   }
restore ability } have to eat/drink the resulting "mix"
haste self      }
enlightenment   }
fooproof               }
enchant weapon/armor   }
remove curse           } use the object as an ingredient
turn undead            }
stone to flesh         }

create a (non-permament, mobile) light-source

It would be very nice to have an action or two which are specific to
sorcery. Perhaps making dragon scale mail? Gaining intrinsic telepathy?
Some new ones?

